  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Check systematically which, if any, of $x+1$, $x-1$, $x+2$, $x-2$, $x+4$ and $x-4$ are factors of each polynomial $P(x)$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$P(x)=x^3+x^2-4x-4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x+1$, $x+2$, $x-2$.\n\nThese give zero remainders; the other listed candidates do not.",
          "worked": "Test the zeros of the candidate factors in the listed order.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-1)&=(-1)^3+(-1)^2-4(-1)-4\\\\P(-1)&=0\\\\P(1)&=1^3+1^2-4(1)-4\\\\P(1)&=-6\\\\P(-2)&=(-2)^3+(-2)^2-4(-2)-4\\\\P(-2)&=0\\\\P(2)&=2^3+2^2-4(2)-4\\\\P(2)&=0\\\\P(-4)&=(-4)^3+(-4)^2-4(-4)-4\\\\P(-4)&=-36\\\\P(4)&=4^3+4^2-4(4)-4\\\\P(4)&=60\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, the listed factors are $x+1$, $x+2$ and $x-2$, because their corresponding evaluations are zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$P(x)=x^4+5x^3+3x^2-5x-4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x+1$, $x-1$, $x+4$.\n\nThese give zero remainders; the other listed candidates do not.",
          "worked": "Test the zeros of the candidate factors in the listed order.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-1)&=(-1)^4+5(-1)^3+3(-1)^2-5(-1)-4\\\\P(-1)&=0\\\\P(1)&=1^4+5(1)^3+3(1)^2-5(1)-4\\\\P(1)&=0\\\\P(-2)&=(-2)^4+5(-2)^3+3(-2)^2-5(-2)-4\\\\P(-2)&=-6\\\\P(2)&=2^4+5(2)^3+3(2)^2-5(2)-4\\\\P(2)&=54\\\\P(-4)&=(-4)^4+5(-4)^3+3(-4)^2-5(-4)-4\\\\P(-4)&=0\\\\P(4)&=4^4+5(4)^3+3(4)^2-5(4)-4\\\\P(4)&=600\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, the listed factors are $x+1$, $x-1$ and $x+4$, because their corresponding evaluations are zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 110
      }
    ]
  },
